# LF everywhere, checked out and committed.
#
# This is not tidiness. Two things in this repository break on CRLF, and both
# break in ways that mislead:
#
#   docker/entrypoint.sh   A shell script with CRLF line endings fails in the
#                          container as "no such file or directory" — naming
#                          the interpreter, not the script — because the \r
#                          becomes part of the shebang path. An operator on
#                          Windows would clone, build, and get an error that
#                          points nowhere near the cause.
#
#   product/*.json         scripts/verify_core_pin.py LF-normalizes text
#                          artifacts before hashing so a Windows checkout and
#                          a Linux one agree. Committing CRLF works, but it
#                          means the bytes on disk are never the bytes that
#                          were hashed, and every future person reasoning
#                          about the pin has to know that.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.sh      text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
Dockerfile     text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile   text eol=lf

*.whl binary
*.png binary
*.zip binary
